{"type": "thinking", "text": "Again! Unchecking Folder first → OK → list still shows Zeta first immediately. So the uncheck does NOT take effect immediately; the list isn't updated. Yet checking it moved Zeta to first immediately (which was already the case)! Wait: before unchecking, Zeta was first because Folder first ON. After unchecking, list still Zeta first (stale). Earlier, after unchecking and then going into an editor and back, Zeta was last (correct). So there is indeed a refresh bug: the sort settings change isn't applied to the file list immediately when turning \"Folder first\" OFF. When turning ON, the result coincidentally matches the stale order (folder first), so it appears to work.\n\nHmm wait, but when I changed sort criterion to Date and Size with folder-first off, the list re-sorted immediately (order changed by date/size) yet Zeta remained first. That suggests the folder grouping was still applied. Then when re-entering, Zeta last. So the fragment's in-memory sort state kept folderFirst=true until recreated. That's a real bug: after changing \"Folder first\", the current list doesn't reflect it until the screen is recreated. Actually it's more general: the sort dialog changes aren't fully applied to the current list view (the directory grouping flag isn't refreshed).\n\nActually, maybe the underlying sequence: The dialog OK triggers only \"sort order\" and \"reverse\" being applied to the adapter, while \"folder first\" is read from prefs by the adapter at creation. Hmm. Either way, bug.\n\nBut wait — when I first changed Name→Date, the list re-sorted by date immediately. So some settings applied live. Then \"Folder first\" didn't. So yes, \"Folder first\" isn't applied to the current view until reopening.\n\nThis is consistent with the change note: \"the directory grouping pass is now applied on every sort\" — maybe the intent was to apply grouping on every sort change. But if the grouping flag is cached at creation, toggling it off doesn't take effect until re-create. Hmm.\n\nI already filed a report about this; it's confirmed. Good. The title as reported says folder grouping applied regardless of setting, which is slightly inaccurate; but the developer confirmed. I'll not file duplicate.\n\nHowever, maybe the true intended bug in this build is different: the sorting comparator itself. Let me check the name sort: Indent2 before Indent, Regex2 before Regex. Wait — with Folder first ON, Name ascending: Indent2, Indent... Hmm, is that correct? Let me examine more carefully. Actually in the current list, order: Indent2.md, Indent.md, Money.md, Notes.md, Regex2.md, Regex.md, Shopping.md, Single.md, todo.txt, Zeta. Hmm, wait Zeta first? In this last observe Zeta is at [8] first because folder first ON.\n\nBut the names: \"Indent2.md\" before \"Indent.md\" and \"Regex2.md\" before \"Regex.md\". Reverse also gave \"Regex, Regex2\"? Earlier reverse listing: todo, Single, Shopping, Regex, Regex2, Notes, Money, Indent, Indent2. So reverse gives Regex before Regex2, i.e., Regex2 > Regex in ascending. So ascending treats \"Regex2.md\" < \"Regex.md\". That's odd for a name sort but might be natural sort treating digits before dot? Eh. In any case, not the target area presumably.\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider: maybe the sort is by name WITHOUT extension and using a \"natural order\" comparator where \"Regex2\" vs \"Regex\"? Natural order: \"Regex\" < \"Regex2\". So ascending should put Regex first. Observed ascending puts Regex2 first → that's descending for this pair. But overall ascending (Indent2... todo.txt... Zeta)? In the current list with folder first ON, Zeta is first so can't tell. Without folder first (from the visit to editor), order was Indent2, Indent, Money, Notes, Regex2, Regex, Shopping, Single, todo, Zeta. If purely name ascending, expected: Indent.md, Indent2.md, Money.md, Notes.md, Regex.md, Regex2.md, Shopping.md, Single.md, todo.txt, Zeta. Observed exactly swapped pairs for Indent/Indent2 and Regex/Regex2. Hmm, that's a systematic pattern: \"2\" before \".\". Could be locale collation \"Indent2\" < \"Indent.md\" because punctuation \".\" is ignored and then comparing \"indent\" vs \"indent2\": shorter first → \"Indent.md\" first. Hmm no.\n\nAlternatively maybe the comparator sorts by filename with extension compared in a way... \"Indent2.md\" vs \"Indent.md\": compare char 6: '2'(50) vs '.'(46) → '2' > '.', so Indent.md first ascending. Observed opposite → descending. But the whole list isn't descending.\n\nHmm, could the app be using \"strcoll\" with punctuation ignored in a weird way. Not the target; skip.\n\nWait, actually: maybe it's because the files' display names are \"Indent2.md\" and \"Indent.md\" but the comparison strips the extension \".md\" leaving \"Indent2\" and \"Indent\"; then uses reverse? \"Indent2\" vs \"Indent\": at index 6, '2' vs end → shorter \"Indent\" first ascending. Observed Indent2 first. So still odd.\n\nUnless the comparator is case-insensitive and uses `compareToIgnoreCase` but with \"Indent2.md\" vs \"Indent.md\"... same result.\n\nHmm, unless the app sorted by \"name\" using natural sort with \"2\" treated as number and \".\" as separator: natural sort \"Indent\" (3) vs \"Indent2\" (2)? Natural comparison might compare \"Indent.\" → \"Indent\" + \"\" vs \"Indent2\" → numeric 2. Empty vs 2 → empty first. Still Indent first.\n\nOK whatever — maybe the displayed list isn't Name sort at all? The dialog said Name checked. Could be a bug where after toggling \"Folder first\" the adapter re-sorts with a stale comparator (e.g., previous Date comparator), coincidentally matching older order? Hmm, the order with folder first off after returning from editor was Indent2, Indent, Money, ... which looks like the original order from the very first observation (Name ascending with folder first ON). So maybe it's truly name sort and the app's name comparison indeed puts Indent2 before Indent. That's plausible if the comparator strips extension and compares from the end? Hmm, e.g., sorting by `lastModified`? no.\n\nLet me test the name sort more directly with a quick check: are there other pairs? \"Shopping.md\" vs \"Single.md\": Shopping < Single (h < i) correct. \"Notes\" < \"Regex\" correct. So only the digit-suffix pairs are swapped. This is consistent with natural sort comparing \"Indent2\" as number 2 vs \"Indent\" with implicit 1? If natural sort treats \"Indent\" as \"Indent\" with no number, and \"Indent2\" with number 2, some implementations order numbered items before unnumbered. That's a known natural-sort behavior. So not a bug.\n\nOK. Enough on sorting. I'll keep testing.\n\nNow the sort bug: I have a confirmed report. The nuance: it's \"not applied until re-enter\". I might file an additional more accurate report? The developer already confirmed the first. Adding a duplicate could be bad. I'll leave it.\n\nNow let's test editing actions and search/replace thoroughly. Open Notes.md (or another file) in edit mode.\n\nActually the current file browser state: folder first OFF. Fine.\n\nLet me open Notes.md again, tap it."}
